The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
struct etr_buf *sysfs_buf = tmc_etr_get_sysfs_buffer(csdev);
...
ret = tmc_etr_enable_hw(drvdata, sysfs_buf);
```
Reason:
The sysfs buffer is allocated (first line) outside the `spinlock` protection. Between that allocation and the subsequent call to `tmc_etr_enable_hw()` (second line), a concurrent perf mode enable can set `drvdata->etr_buf` inside the spinlock. When `tmc_etr_enable_hw()` is later called, it hits the `WARN_ON(drvdata->etr_buf)` because the buffer is already configured by perf mode.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (coresight_get_mode(csdev) == CS_MODE_PERF) {
    drvdata->sysfs_buf = NULL;
    raw_spin_unlock_irqrestore(&drvdata->spinlock, flags);
    tmc_etr_free_sysfs_buf(sysfs_buf);
    return -EBUSY;
}
```
Reason:
After acquiring the spinlock (but before enabling hardware), the fix checks whether the sink is already in perf mode. If so, it cleans up the sysfs buffer (frees it and sets `drvdata->sysfs_buf = NULL`) and returns `-EBUSY`. This prevents `tmc_etr_enable_hw()` from being called when the hardware is already owned by perf mode, eliminating the race condition.

